   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to including extreme winter\nweather preparedness in local comprehensive emergency management plans",
    "basePrintNo": "S4175",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4175-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 55,
       "fullName": "Samra Brouk",
       "imgName": "1317_Samra_Brouk.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1500,
       "sessionMemberId": 2050,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "BROUK"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 48,
       "fullName": "Rachel May",
       "imgName": "1057_Rachel_May.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1237,
       "sessionMemberId": 2023,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "MAY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§20 & 23, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4175\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to including extreme\nwinter weather preparedness in local comprehensive emergency management\nplans\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the definition of a \"disaster\" to include extreme\nwinter weather and would require that local comprehensive emergency\nmanagement plans include plans for extreme winter weather, including\nevacuation plans and sheltering-in-place plans, along with how to deal\nwith transit networks, utilities, and community warming centers. Addi-\ntionally it provides for an annual inventory of the municipality's\nextreme winter weather equipment, including the age and condition of\nsuch equipment.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one adds \"extreme winter weather\" to the definition of \"disas-\nter\" in paragraph A of subdivision 2 of section 20 of the executive law,\nas amended by chapter 23 of the laws of 2020.\n\nSection two amends section 23 of the executive law by adding a new\nsubdivision 8 and calls for local comprehensive emergency management\nplans to provide for extreme winter weather planning, including evacu-\nation plans and sheltering-in-place plans, along with the management of\ntransit networks, utilities, and community warming centers. Additionally\nit provides for an annual inventory of the municipality's extreme winter\nweather equipment, including the age and condition of such equipment.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAll parts of New York State are affected by extreme winter weather from\ntime to time. In some portions of Upstate New York, large cities are\nregularly among the top snowfall recipients in the nation.  Addi-\ntionally, as climate change continues to impact our weather outcomes,\nextreme winter weather events are becoming more commonplace. For exam-\nple, during the 2022-23 Winter, Lake Erie was largely unfrozen. In\nregions like Western New York, that means that the plentiful source of\nsurface water that is necessary to generate lake-effect snow remained in\nplace throughout the season, bypassing the traditional end/decrease of\nlake-effect snow in the region that occurs when Lake Erie freezes.\n\nRecently, in December 2022, a historic blizzard hit the Buffalo Metro\nRegion. The blizzard, which lasted for multiple dates, had wind gusts of\nnearly 80 miles per hour, dropped up to four feet of snow on the region,\nwhich then drifted into massive piles that quickly covered vehicles,\nvents, electric substations, and more, knocked out power to tens of\nthousands of homes and places of business, and, tragically, led to the\ndeaths of at least 47 individuals from a variety of causes. In addition\nto this horrific loss of life, tens of thousands were left without elec-\ntricity in the midst of extreme cold temperatures, and at least two of\nthe warming centers set up in the City of Buffalo for residents also\nlost power. Travel was next-to-impossible, with whiteout conditions\ncontinuing for dozens of hours upon end, and, for the first time in its\nhistory, the Buffalo Fire Department was, for a period of time, unable\nto respond to any emergency calls.\n\nWhile storms of this magnitude were previously considered to be genera-\ntional storms that occur only once or twice a century, climate change is\nunfortunately making them much more common. As a result, municipalities\nin New York State need to ensure that they have plans and resources in\nplace to address these historic, but now more common, storms. This bill\nwill ensure that municipalities across New York State take extreme\nwinter weather into account when formulating and updating their disaster\npreparedness plans.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis law shall take effect immediately after it shall become a law.\n",
